\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Closed weights are the divergence-free ones}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and let #{\Omega^1} be a
free calculus of \ref{fdip-00NU} whose maximal prolongation has no
#{2}-form, as the directed cycle of length at least #{3} does by
\ref{fdip-00NZ}. A weight assigns #{w_{xy}\in k} to each edge. The
\newvocab{weighted integral} of a #{1}-form
#{\omega=\sum\omega_{xy}e_{xy}} is
##{\int\omega=\sum_{x\to y}w_{xy}\omega_{xy}.}
The integral is \newvocab{closed} when #{\int \covwedge f=0} for every
function #{f}. This holds if and only if, at every vertex, the sum
of the weights on incoming edges equals the sum of the weights on
outgoing edges. For the actions of \ref{fdip-00NT},
##{\int f\omega-\int\omega f
=\sum_{x\to y}w_{xy}\bigl(f(x)-f(y)\bigr)\omega_{xy}.}
The difference vanishes for all #{f} and #{\omega} if and only if
every weight is zero. A nonzero closed integral is therefore not an
untwisted graded trace in the sense of \citef{connes1985ncg}. On
the directed cycle the constant weight #{1} is closed, and the
difference reduces to #{\sum_i\bigl(f(i)-f(i+1)\bigr)\omega_i}.}

\proof{
\p{Substitute #{\covwedge f}. The coefficient of #{f(z)} in #{\int \covwedge f} is
the incoming sum at #{z} minus the outgoing sum at #{z}. The
integral vanishes for every #{f} exactly when each of those
coefficients is zero.}

\p{Left multiplication by #{f} multiplies the coefficient on
#{x\to y} by #{f(x)}, and right multiplication multiplies it by
#{f(y)}. Their integrals differ by the displayed sum. Take
#{\omega=e_{xy}} and #{f=\delta_x}. The sum reduces to #{w_{xy}}.
Thus every coefficient vanishes. The constant weight on a directed
cycle has one incoming edge and one outgoing edge at each vertex,
so it is closed, and the same substitution gives the cycle formula.}
}
